---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Operations Overview', href: '/docs/operations', active: true },
  { title: 'Two-Host', href: '/docs/operations/two-host' },
  { title: 'Single-Host', href: '/docs/operations/single-host' },
  { title: 'Hardening', href: '/docs/operations/hardening' },
  { title: 'Runbooks', href: '/docs/operations/runbooks' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'domains', text: 'Operational Domains', level: 2 },
  { id: 'topologies', text: 'Deployment Topologies', level: 2 },
  { id: 'routing', text: 'Routing, Pooling and Identity', level: 2 },
  { id: 'config', text: 'Configuration Reference', level: 2 },
  { id: 'honest', text: 'Honest Assessment', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Operations" description="Deployment, hardening, configuration and runbooks for the honeypot system" section="Operations" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    Operations covers the parts that decide whether detection survives a bad day: how the two stacks are
    placed, which credentials isolate the pools, what the router does when state is missing, and how to
    recover without guessing. The defaults below match <code>ids/.env.example</code> and are safe to start
    from, not to leave unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2 id="domains">Operational Domains</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Deployment</strong>: Two-host and single-host topologies.</li>
    <li><strong>Deception layer</strong>: Choosing the <a href="/docs/engine/layers">honeypot layer</a> — reverse-proxy pools (default, isolates file/upload/RCE as well as data-layer attacks) or the lighter database layer (one eshop, DB swapped per request).</li>
    <li><strong>Monitoring</strong>: Observability, dashboards, alerting.</li>
    <li><strong>Hardening</strong>: Production security recommendations.</li>
    <li><strong>Runbooks</strong>: Troubleshooting and incident procedures.</li>
    <li><strong>Routing & Pooling</strong>: Router decision pipeline, honeypot pool assignment, session management.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="topologies">Deployment Topologies</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard
      title="Two-host (production shape)"
      href="/docs/operations/two-host"
      icon="server"
      tag="Recommended"
      description="Edge runs the proxy, shop, pools, Redis, Suricata and Vector. SIEM runs Elasticsearch, Kibana and the aggregator behind an outbound-only mTLS channel."
      meta="Best isolation"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Single-host"
      href="/docs/operations/single-host"
      icon="box"
      tag="Evaluation"
      description="Every component on one machine. Simplest to run; resource budgets, load and disk guards matter much more."
      meta="Lab and evaluation"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Hardening"
      href="/docs/operations/hardening"
      icon="shield"
      tag="Before exposure"
      description="Whitelists, session signing, decay settings, AbuseIPDB limits, diagnostic header gating and network controls."
      meta="Checklist"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Runbooks"
      href="/docs/operations/runbooks"
      icon="wrench"
      tag="During incidents"
      description="Service restart, incident response, common failure modes and escalation with the evidence an operator needs."
      meta="Step-by-step"
    />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="routing">Routing, Pooling and Identity</h2>
  <p>How a request is routed, how a session is bound to a pool and how the consent-gated signature works are
  described once, where they are designed:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/architecture#pipeline">Request Pipeline</a> — the routing stages in <code>router.lua</code>, in order.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/architecture#pooling">Pooling System</a> and <a href="/docs/engine">Honeypot Engine</a> — exclusive assignment, spare provisioning, borrowing, reuse at the resource limit and idle retirement.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/architecture/lua-internals#assign">Lua Internals → Pool Assignment</a> — the Redis keys and atomic claim behind it.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/detection/signature">Device &amp; GDPR Signature</a> — the full consent-gated identity flow and its settings.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2 id="config">Configuration Reference (from init.lua)</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Section</th><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    <tr><td>threat</td><td>honeypot_threshold</td><td>80</td><td>Minimum score for honeypot routing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>threat</td><td>max_risk_score</td><td>100</td><td>Hard cap on accumulated score</td></tr>
    <tr><td>threat</td><td>ip_reputation_cap</td><td>10</td><td>Max reputation contribution on shared addresses</td></tr>
    <tr><td>threat</td><td>score_decay_half_life_seconds</td><td>300s</td><td>Half-life for score decay (0 = disabled)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>threat</td><td>decay_offense_slowdown</td><td>0.75</td><td>Half-life multiplier per offense</td></tr>
    <tr><td>threat</td><td>permaflag_offenses</td><td>5</td><td>Offenses at/above this → no decay</td></tr>
    <tr><td>threat</td><td>permaflag_score</td><td>100</td><td>Score at/above this → permaflag</td></tr>
    <tr><td>session</td><td>max_idle_time</td><td>3600s</td><td>Session TTL</td></tr>
    <tr><td>session</td><td>tamper_score</td><td>50</td><td>Score added for tampered cookies</td></tr>
    <tr><td>abuseipdb</td><td>confidence_minimum</td><td>90</td><td>Bulk feed confidence threshold</td></tr>
    <tr><td>abuseipdb</td><td>daily_check_limit</td><td>1000</td><td>Free-tier daily cap on on-demand checks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>abuseipdb</td><td>blacklist_enabled</td><td>false</td><td>Bulk blacklist pull opt-in</td></tr>
    <tr><td>internal_test_secret</td><td>—</td><td>from env</td><td>Gates diagnostic headers (fail closed when unset)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Explainable detection, pure/adapter split, per-adversary isolation, elastic pools, no tarpit, privacy-preserving identity, honeytoken injection.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> Same-host testing gap, decay complexity, NAT protection trade-off, admin-ajax complexity, pool scaling limits, threshold tuning, no dashboard health monitoring, honeytoken coverage (5 types), rule-based detection, Redis dependency, GDPR key requirement.</p>
</DocsLayout>
